				<div id="essay">
					<h2>A gymnast who gives it her all</h2>
					<p>Gymnastics is where Dakota shines. The early indications were there; she was born two months early after spinning crazily in her mother&#8217;s belly, did the splits in her sleep, and climbed countertops before walking. Gymnastics seemed like the logical choice for a bouncing three-year-old to burn off excess energy.</p>
					<p class="pullquote">Dakota takes gymnastics seriously, yet what matters more is that she does her best.</p>
					<p>More than six years later, she&#8217;s still at it, and doing more every year. She spends three or four nights a week at practice and frequently competes on weekends. In fact, she would spend every night practicing if her parents didn&#8217;t insist on a break.</p>
					<p>Dakota works non-stop, focusing not only on where she excels, but just as much on weaker events, such as her floor routine. Nothing makes her happier than mastering a new skill. She knows that if she does it over and over, it will keep getting better&#8212;whether it takes just days, as the tuck did, or months, like her kip. She is confident that with enough work, it will fall into place.</p>
					<p>Although Dakota takes her sport seriously, what matters more is that she does her best&#8212;and if somebody else scores higher, she is happy for them. She just keeps working harder to improve her scores.</p>
					<p>Another lesson Dakota has taken to heart from gymnastics is the importance of supporting teammates. She participates in the group&#8217;s spirited cheers and is the first to comfort a teammate after a mistakeor to congratulate them for a winning score. Her teammates are her friends before they are competition.</p>
					<p class="last">However, her most admirable quality is her support for the younger gymnasts.  She demonstrates skills and helps them learn, making them feel like part of the team and offering support to those who might not have the confidence to know how good they can be.  Who woudn&#8217;t be proud of that?</p>
				</div>
